  "result": "`summarize()` now handles 200,000 orders well under a second, and `npm test` passes (4 of 4). The output is unchanged, including ordering.\n\n**Cause.** There were three quadratic or worse costs: `seen.includes` for deduping, `groups.find` for customer lookup, and a full re-sort of all groups after every order.\n\n**Fix in `src/report.js`:**\n- **Dedupe and lookup:** `seen` is now a `Set` and groups live in a `Map` keyed by customer.\n- **Sorting:** the sort runs once at the end instead of per order. `byRank` is a total order over distinct customers, so the final order is the same.\n- **Unchanged:** refunded orders still count as seen for dedupe, and the returned objects keep the same shape.\n\n**Tests added in `test/report.test.js`:**\n- **Oracle comparison:** compares `summarize` against a copy of the original implementation. The data has duplicate ids, refunds, EUR orders, and many ties in spend and first-order time. It runs several limits plus the empty-input case, and `deepStrictEqual` checks the output, so a change in output or ordering would fail it.\n- **Speed check:** a 200,000-order test asserts the run takes under 1 second.\n\nI didn't print the actual timing. The old code did not finish the test suite within 120 s on this data, so I stopped it.\n\nOne edge I didn't handle: a `NaN` customer id would be grouped differently by `Map` than by the old `===` lookup. It looks unrealistic for customer ids.",
